# How many pages each program of this corpus draws. One basename and
# one count per line.
#
# A run compares the pages both engines drew, so a page neither of them
# drew is not in the comparison and is not missing from either side of
# it. A program that stops emitting partway through reports every page
# it reached as a match and says nothing whatever about the rest. This
# is what says how many pages there were to reach, so the run can name
# the ones it never got to.
#
# It is held from both ends: a program of this corpus with no count
# here fails the corpus, so one cannot be added without a count, and a
# count naming a program the corpus does not hold fails it too.
#
# The counts are not a record of what this tree draws -- a count taken
# from that makes whatever the tree does today correct by definition.
# These are listings out of two books, and one of the sixty-six carries
# a %%Pages: line; the %%Page: comments are worse than nothing, since
# two of the ten programs that have any draw no page at all. So the
# source of every count here is the listing, read to each point at
# which it asks for a page.
#
# Counting the word showpage is not that reading, and it is wrong four
# times over.
#
#   bb_13 writes showpage once and draws six. The one it writes is
#   inside two nested for loops, and the call at the foot of the file
#   asks for three columns by two rows: a poster is a single drawing
#   spread over the sheets it takes, which is the whole point of the
#   program.
#
#   gb_11-01 writes it twice and draws one. The first is a definition
#   and not a call -- the prologue defines showpage to do nothing, so
#   that a document included in this one cannot eject the including
#   document's page. That definition is undone by the restore that ends
#   the inclusion, three lines above the second showpage, so the second
#   is the operator and the page is this document's own.
#
#   gb_04-02 writes two and gb_04-04 writes two, gb_04-03 three, and
#   each reaches one. They are page-breaking emulators, and their extra
#   showpages are the break: a line below the bottom margin, a line too
#   long for the buffer that reads it, a word past the right edge with
#   no room left below. Each carries the text it sets in its own tail,
#   so whether it breaks is arithmetic and not a guess. gb_04-02 sets
#   four lines of Shakespeare at ten point leading from a top of 720
#   against a bottom of 72; gb_04-03 four lines at fourteen point
#   leading, none of them near the thousand-character buffer that would
#   trip the other break; gb_04-04 fifteen words of 48-point Times from
#   y=700, wrapping when a word would pass x=300 and dropping 48 each
#   time it does, which puts the last of them at 412 against a break at
#   36. None of the three ever reaches its break, and each draws the
#   one page its last line asks for.
#
# Thirteen have no page in the comparison, twelve because the listing
# asks for none and one because it is stopped before it asks. nopage
# carries the reason for each, one at a time.
